# VnV review profile — output schema

The exact markdown shape `/vnv-review` emits. Consuming reviewers rely on this structure, so do not reorder sections or rename headings.

## Template

```markdown
# VnV Review Profile

**Scope:** <scope-spec> — N files, M commits.
**Generated:** <ISO datetime>.

## Subsystem map

| Subsystem | Files |
|---|---|
| RustAPI | command_node/rust/crates/bto-api/src/auth.rs, … |
| Frontend | frontend/apps/terminal/components/Foo.tsx, … |
| (unmapped) | path/that/did/not/match.txt |

## Must-run commands

Lifted from each matched subsystem's `<guidance-root>/<Subsystem>/AGENTS.md`
"Tools" section. Run all of these.

### RustAPI
- cargo clippy -D warnings
- cargo test -p bto-api
- cargo audit
- (additional verbatim from RustAPI/AGENTS.md)

### Frontend
- pnpm biome check
- pnpm tsc --noEmit
- pnpm test
- (additional verbatim from Frontend/AGENTS.md)

## Optional deeper tools

References to load on demand. Reviewers read only what they need; do not pre-load.

### From `<guidance-root>/SECURITY_TOOLS.md`
- § Rust → cargo-geiger, noseyparker, binsec, rust-san
- § API Security → ZAP API scan, schemathesis, cherrybomb
- § Docker & Container Security → hadolint, trivy, dockle

### From `<guidance-root>/PERFORMANCE_TOOLS.md`
- § Rust → flamegraph, tokio-console, criterion
- § API/HTTP → k6, wrk2, Tempo tracing

### From `<guidance-root>/SEO_GEO_CHECKLIST.md`
- (only if Frontend matched) — full checklist applies to every frontend review

## Threat-model prompts

(Only present if triggered. One-liner per trigger. Reviewers expand as needed.)

- New HTTP endpoint detected at `command_node/rust/crates/bto-api/src/routes/foo.rs` — auth path? rate-limit? input validation?
- New external integration in `…/stripe_webhook.rs` — signature verification? failure modes? PII flow?

## Findings target

- RustAPI findings → the target declared by its subsystem guidance
- Frontend findings → the target declared by its subsystem guidance
- (unmapped findings) → report as `unclassified` in the active findings output until subsystem Scope is updated; include unresolved items in the plan closeout summary

## VnV rules in force

These apply to any work the reviewer does on the basis of this profile:

1. Do not modify SARIF files (raw scanner output, date-stamped).
2. Findings format: rule_id | path | severity | status | date.
3. No proprietary info in findings or reports (no model names, scoring formulas).
4. Include any additional lane rule declared by the matched subsystem guidance.

(Rules 1, 2, 3 are from the discovered VnV root guidance. Additional rules are
included only when the matched subsystem guidance declares them.)

## Evidence routing

- **Guidance root:** `<docs/vnv or Vnv>`
- **Evidence root:** `<declared Vnv path, same as guidance root, or none>`

---

## Notes

- The "Must-run commands" section is **verbatim** from the subsystem AGENTS.md's "Tools" list. If the AGENTS.md is wrong, the fix goes there.
- The "Optional deeper tools" section names section headings only — never inlines body text. Reviewers grep into the referenced doc on demand.
- An empty "Threat-model prompts" section is omitted entirely, not left blank.
- The profile is markdown so reviewers read it directly. Do not switch to JSON without updating its consumers.

- `docs/developer-feedback/observations.md` is the running log. It is
  public per SD-011.
  - One row per observation, with the fields of the issue #1 Scribe
    contract.
  - Seeded with DX-001..DX-007, the planning observations and the Phase 1
    observations (S08).
- Log schema (AC-AFC-DX-04, DEC-AFC-SD-025). The columns follow the Devpost
  rules (`evidence/devpost-feedback-rules.md`, fetched with HTTP 200 on
  2026-10-12), so every entry is complete when it is written:

  | Table | Columns |
  |---|---|
  | Observations: issue #1 | ID, Timestamp, Reporting role, Task or packet, Tool/API/SDK, Version, Device/OS, Evidence, Classification |
  | Observations: Product Feedback | Used for, What worked well, What needs work, Onboarding (zero to hello world), Would build again (Yes/No and why), AWS services used and how |
  | Observations: Friction Log Entry | Task attempted, Steps taken, Expected result, Actual result, Severity rating, Workaround used, Actionable suggestion |
  | Feature requests | ID, Tool, Description, Why it matters to this project, Priority |

  - The log has two headings, `## Observations` and `## Feature requests`,
    each followed by its table. Column names are exactly as above.
  - Priority is Critical, Important or Nice-to-have, as the rules list.
  - The observations table is one table with all three column groups.
    A cell that does not apply holds `n/a`. A value nobody measured holds
    `unknown`. The Scribe never invents a value.
  - "Expected vs. actual result" is two columns. The Phase 2 assembly
    joins them.
  - Severity rating uses Blocker, Major, Minor or None (a positive
    experience). The rules name no scale, so this one is a design choice.
  - AWS services used and how is filled only for AWS tools. It feeds the
    AWS Builder Mini Challenge answer inside Product Feedback.
- Submission assembly is Phase 2 work, done at submission time from these
  rows. It produces per-tool Product Feedback, optional Feature Requests and
  sanitized Friction Log entries. Stage 1 can recommend a bonus of up to 10%
  for friction log entries.
- Every packet template in `implementation.json` carries the
  `developer_feedback` section (AC-AFC-DX-01).
- Lenses: Developer feedback, Owner boundary.

